<Guide slug="tayammum" title="Tayammum" description="When tayammum replaces wuḍūʾ while travelling, how it is done, and where the four schools differ, with sources." lede="Tayammum is purification with clean earth instead of water. It is a mercy for the traveller, and it is for when there is no water or you can’t use it. At most airports there is water, so tayammum is the exception, not the routine." sources={sources}>
  <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
  <p>Two verses mention it by name. If you are ill or on a journey, and you find no water, “seek clean earth and wipe over your faces and hands” <span class="cite">(Qurʾān 4:43; 5:6)</span>. The second verse adds: “Allah does not intend to make difficulty for you.” The Prophet ﷺ said the earth had been made for him a place of prayer and a means of purification <span class="cite">(Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī 335)</span>.</p>

  <h2>When it applies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>No water:</b> there is genuinely none you can reach, after a reasonable search <span class="cite">(Qurʾān 4:43)</span>.</li>
    <li><b>Water you can’t use:</b> for example illness, an injury that water would harm, or cold so severe that washing would put you in danger, with no way to warm the water. ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ made tayammum on a very cold night, and the Prophet ﷺ did not blame him <span class="cite">(Sunan Abī Dāwūd 334)</span>. When an injured traveller was told he had to bathe and died, the Prophet ﷺ strongly criticised those who told him <span class="cite">(Sunan Abī Dāwūd 336)</span>.</li>
    <li><b>Water you need to drink:</b> Ibn Qudāmah cites Ibn al-Mundhir’s report that the scholars agree a traveller who fears thirst keeps the water for drinking and makes tayammum <span class="cite">(al-Mughnī 1:195)</span>. The schools’ own texts say the same <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:29; Khalīl p. 24; Minhāj p. 17)</span>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Not wanting to wash in public is not on this list. Where there is water you can use, the schools require wuḍūʾ. See <a href="/travel/wudu-at-the-airport/">wuḍūʾ at the airport</a> for ways to make it quick and discreet.</p>
  <p>What if there is water, but using it would mean missing the prayer’s time, for example because of a very long queue? The schools discuss this. Al-Hidāyah says that someone who fears the time will pass if he makes wuḍūʾ still makes wuḍūʾ, and makes up the prayer <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:29)</span>. Khalīl records a disagreement within the Mālikī school on whether that fear allows tayammum <span class="cite">(Khalīl p. 24)</span>. Ask your local scholar before relying on either.</p>

  <h2>How it is done</h2>
  <p>ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir described what the Prophet ﷺ showed him: he struck the earth with his hands, blew into them, then wiped his face and his hands <span class="cite">(Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī 338)</span>. The schools’ texts all require an intention and wiping the face and the hands with clean earth <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:28; Khalīl p. 25; Minhāj p. 17)</span>. They differ on how many strikes, how far up the arm, and what counts as earth:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Ḥanafī:</b> two strikes, one to wipe the face and one to wipe the arms to the elbows <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:27)</span>.</li>
    <li><b>Mālikī:</b> wiping the face and the hands to the wrists is required; wiping to the elbows and a second strike for the arms are sunnah <span class="cite">(Khalīl pp. 24–25)</span>. Al-Risālah describes the full form, with a second strike and wiping to the elbows <span class="cite">(al-Risālah p. 21)</span>.</li>
    <li><b>Shāfiʿī:</b> the face, then the arms with the elbows; al-Nawawī states that two strikes are required <span class="cite">(Minhāj pp. 17–18)</span>.</li>
    <li><b>Ḥanbalī:</b> the sunnah is one strike, for the face and the hands; two strikes are also valid <span class="cite">(al-Mughnī 1:179)</span>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where the schools differ</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Tayammum by school" tabindex="0">
    <table class="schools">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">School</th><th scope="col">Strikes and hands</th><th scope="col">What counts as earth</th><th scope="col">How long it lasts</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Ḥanafī</th><td>Two strikes: one for the face, one for the arms to the elbows <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:27)</span></td><td>Anything of the earth’s kind, such as soil, sand and stone (Abū Ḥanīfah and Muḥammad; Abū Yūsuf: soil and sand only) <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:28)</span></td><td>As many obligatory and voluntary prayers as you like, until something breaks it <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:29)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Mālikī</th><td>One strike and wiping to the wrists are required; a second strike and wiping to the elbows are sunnah <span class="cite">(Khalīl p. 25)</span></td><td>What is on the face of the earth: soil, sand, stone, salt flats; soil is best <span class="cite">(al-Risālah p. 21; Khalīl p. 25)</span></td><td>One obligatory prayer per tayammum <span class="cite">(al-Risālah p. 21; Khalīl p. 24)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Shāfiʿī</th><td>Two strikes; the arms are wiped with the elbows <span class="cite">(Minhāj pp. 17–18)</span></td><td>Pure soil (turāb), or sand that has dust in it <span class="cite">(Minhāj p. 17)</span></td><td>One obligatory prayer per tayammum, and any number of voluntary ones <span class="cite">(Minhāj p. 18)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Ḥanbalī</th><td>One strike for the face and hands is the sunnah, following ʿAmmār’s report <span class="cite">(al-Mughnī 1:179)</span></td><td>Only pure soil with dust that sticks to the hand <span class="cite">(al-Mughnī 1:182)</span></td><td>Until the prayer’s time ends <span class="cite">(al-Mughnī 1:193)</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What ends it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Anything that breaks wuḍūʾ also breaks tayammum <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:28; Khalīl p. 25)</span>.</li>
    <li>Finding water you are able to use ends it, because the reason for it has gone <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:28)</span>. The schools differ on what happens if you find water during or after the prayer <span class="cite">(Khalīl p. 25; Minhāj p. 18; al-Mughnī 1:179)</span>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>At the airport and on board</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Airports and planes nearly always have water, so wuḍūʾ is usually possible, even if it takes some planning.</li>
    <li>Whether a dusty wall or a seat counts as “earth” depends on the school, as the table shows. Ask your local scholar before relying on it.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
